    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 211823\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1123 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- The overall pattern will favor dry and tranquil conditions\n  across the Desert Southwest through at least the Thanksgiving \n  Weekend. \n  \n- Temperatures over the next several days will average a few\n  degrees below normal before a warming trend pushes readings to\n  above seasonal levels by the back half of next week. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nUpper level troughing continues over the Desert SW as the \nshortwave continues to push eastwards, and is forecasted to be in\nthe Southern Plains by early Sunday morning. As this system \npasses it will keep H5 heights lower, between 576-570 dam, \nresulting in below normal temperatures today, with highs expected \nto be in the upper 60's with Phoenix having about a 50% chance of \nstaying below 70 degrees. If Phoenix does stay below the 70 \ndegree mark it would be the first time since February 20th that a \nsub-70 high temperatures is seen at the station. \n\nBy Sunday the shortwave will be into the Plain States, as \nmentioned above, however morning low temperatures will continue to\nbe in the mid to upper 40s across the lower deserts and even into\nthe 30s in the higher terrain areas, where a Freeze Warning \nremains in effect for eastern Gila County Sunday morning. With the\npassing of the shortwave, heights will begin to increase \nslightly, but will remain under broad troughing. This will lead to\nafternoon high temperatures climbing back into the low to mid 70s\nfor Sunday, just around normal for late November. By Monday \nmorning another troughing feature, albeit weaker, will quickly \npass through Arizona resulting in temperatures cooling 1F-2F, \nbefore upper level ridging begins to build behind the \naforementioned trough. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT SATURDAY/...  \nNot much in the way of change can be expected for the longer term \noutlook as the broader flow pattern promotes a continuation of \nbenign conditions. Model guidance points towards a slight rise in \nheights aloft as we progress through the middle portion of the week, \nlikely initiating a gradual warming trend for the region during this \ntimeframe. A greater upward trend in atmospheric heights is shown \ntaking place by Thanksgiving as an unseasonably strong area of high \npressure sets up west of the Baja Peninsula. The good news is, the \nposition of this ridge will put the Desert Southwest under the \ninfluence of cooler northwesterly flow, which will likely prevent\ntemperatures that are completely out of the ordinary for the end \nof November. Even though above-normal readings will make a return \nas early as Wednesday, daily highs during the back half of the \nweek should remain quite pleasant with lower desert readings in \nthe middle to upper 70s, with perhaps a few spots reaching the \nlower 80s by next weekend. With that being said however, it would \nnot be surprising to see forecasted highs for Thursday and onward\ntrend higher from their current position as the NBM tends to be \non the cooler side 6-10 days out.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1749Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nLight W'rly winds, aob 6 kts, are favored through this evening\nfollowed by an E'rly shift tonight. Periods of light variability \nand calm winds are also expected. FEW to SCT high clouds will \nfilter into the region this evening and FEW cumulus around 6K ft \nAGL will develop Sunday afternoon. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nWinds will maintain a W/N component through the TAF period with\nperiods of light variability and calm winds as well. SCT high\nclouds will spread across the area this afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER... \nDry and tranquil conditions will be common across the region\nthrough the next several days. Outside of a slight spike on\nMonday, MinRHs values will hover between 15-30% through most of \nthe forecast period, while cool overnight lows will result in \ndecent to good overnight recovery. Winds overall are expected to \nbe light and favor diurnal trends. Temperatures will hover near to\nslightly below normal levels through the front half of the \nupcoming week ahead of slight warming later in the forecast \nperiod.\n\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...Updated 11/21 1805\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area due to water releases from the dam. \nWater releases remain around 3000 cfs. Water has now reached the\nDateland area with a streamflow gauge reporting just under 800 \ncfs. CBRFC's latest forecast for this gauge has water rising above\naction stage (8 ft) on Sunday. A new Flood Warning segment now\nextends from Dateland to Yuma. These water levels will have the \npotential to flood farm fields and low water crossings along the \nriver downstream from the dam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Freeze Warning from midnight tonight to 9 AM MST Sunday for \n     AZZ563.\n\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...RW \nAVIATION...Benedict \nFIRE WEATHER...RW/Ryan \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero/Benedict\n",
